3
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:09,360
through the brilliant, incisive eyes of Rashi, the

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,680
great medieval commentator.

5
00:00:11,808 --> 00:00:14,322
This week we're looking at the special Torah

6
00:00:14,382 --> 00:00:17,042
reading for the first day of Sukkot, drawn

7
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:20,882
from Leviticus chapters twenty-two and twenty-three.

8
00:00:20,989 --> 00:00:24,162
It's a rich and dense portion, and Rashi

9
00:00:24,202 --> 00:00:26,482
has a lot to say, so let's dive

10
00:00:26,562 --> 00:00:32,270
in.This Torah reading covers some truly foundational ground.

11
00:00:32,537 --> 00:00:35,070
It begins with laws about the fitness of

12
00:00:35,116 --> 00:00:38,430
animal sacrifices, including the minimum age of an

13
00:00:38,499 --> 00:00:40,990
animal before it can be offered, and the

14
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:44,190
prohibition against slaughtering a mother animal and its

15
00:00:44,243 --> 00:00:45,630
young on the same day.

16
00:00:45,822 --> 00:00:48,190
Then it moves into one of the great

17
00:00:48,252 --> 00:00:52,192
chapters of the Torah: Leviticus twenty-three, which lays

18
00:00:52,252 --> 00:00:55,552
out the entire calendar of Jewish festivals, from

19
00:00:55,605 --> 00:00:58,592
Shabbat to Passover, from the counting of the

20
00:00:58,752 --> 00:01:02,592
Omer to Shavuot, from Rosh Hashanah to Yom

21
00:01:02,632 --> 00:01:06,432
Kippur, and finally to Sukkot itself.

22
00:01:06,592 --> 00:01:09,472
It's essentially God's appointment book with the Jewish

23
00:01:09,541 --> 00:01:10,032
people.

24
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:14,032
What's fascinating about Rashi's approach here is how

25
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:17,392
many of his comments address technical legal questions:

26
00:01:17,568 --> 00:01:20,672
what exactly does a word mean, what is

27
00:01:20,734 --> 00:01:24,272
included or excluded by a particular phrase, and

28
00:01:24,312 --> 00:01:28,032
how do we reconcile apparent repetitions or contradictions

29
00:01:28,085 --> 00:01:28,912
in the text.

30
00:01:29,152 --> 00:01:33,952
But alongside these precise legal observations, Rashi also

31
00:01:34,019 --> 00:01:37,392
draws out moments of stunning emotional and spiritual

32
00:01:37,485 --> 00:01:40,592
depth, reminding us that behind the laws there

33
00:01:40,619 --> 00:01:45,244
is a living relationship between God and Israel.Let

34
00:01:45,297 --> 00:01:47,484
me walk you through some of Rashi's most

35
00:01:47,546 --> 00:01:49,324
striking comments on this portion.

36
00:01:49,591 --> 00:01:52,124
We'll start near the beginning, with the law

37
00:01:52,177 --> 00:01:56,044
found in Leviticus twenty-two, verse twenty-eight, which prohibits

38
00:01:56,104 --> 00:01:58,364
slaughtering an animal and its young on the

39
00:01:58,428 --> 00:01:59,164
same day.

40
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:03,245
The Torah uses the phrase oto v'et b'no,

41
00:02:03,325 --> 00:02:06,925
literally "it and its son," using a masculine

42
00:02:06,989 --> 00:02:08,285
pronoun, oto.

43
00:02:08,605 --> 00:02:11,805
Rashi immediately notices something odd.

44
00:02:12,098 --> 00:02:14,845
If the law were about the father animal,

45
00:02:14,865 --> 00:02:17,645
the masculine pronoun would make perfect sense.

46
00:02:17,805 --> 00:02:21,085
But Rashi tells us, based on the Talmud,

47
00:02:21,133 --> 00:02:23,405
that this law actually applies only to the

48
00:02:23,462 --> 00:02:25,245
mother animal and its offspring.

49
00:02:25,425 --> 00:02:27,565
You may not slaughter the dam and her

50
00:02:27,632 --> 00:02:29,885
young on the same day, but a father

51
00:02:29,954 --> 00:02:32,205
animal and its offspring may be slaughtered on

52
00:02:32,245 --> 00:02:33,165
the same day.

53
00:02:33,425 --> 00:02:34,285
Why?

54
00:02:34,395 --> 00:02:37,885
Because with a mother, the parentage is certain,

55
00:02:37,925 --> 00:02:40,205
while with a father, it often isn't.

56
00:02:40,525 --> 00:02:42,861
So Rashi is teaching us that the Torah

57
00:02:42,862 --> 00:02:46,546
sometimes uses a grammatical form that doesn't match

58
00:02:46,586 --> 00:02:49,506
the legal reality, and a careful reader needs

59
00:02:49,533 --> 00:02:51,906
to know when the text is speaking loosely.

60
00:02:52,173 --> 00:02:55,106
It's a perfect example of how Rashi reads

61
00:02:55,186 --> 00:02:58,626
every word, even a single pronoun, as carrying

62
00:02:58,706 --> 00:03:02,672
legal significance.Now let's jump ahead to the festival

63
00:03:02,743 --> 00:03:03,792
calendar.

64
00:03:03,899 --> 00:03:07,396
In Leviticus twenty-three, verse three, the Torah mentions

65
00:03:07,476 --> 00:03:10,676
Shabbat right before listing all the holidays.

66
00:03:10,863 --> 00:03:14,676
Rashi asks a great question: what does Shabbat

67
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:16,516
have to do with the festivals?

68
00:03:16,703 --> 00:03:20,196
Shabbat is fixed, it comes every seven days

69
00:03:20,249 --> 00:03:21,316
no matter what.

70
00:03:21,436 --> 00:03:24,836
The festivals, by contrast, were historically dependent on

71
00:03:24,876 --> 00:03:28,116
the Jewish court, the Sanhedrin, which proclaimed the

72
00:03:28,196 --> 00:03:30,756
new months and determined when the holidays would

73
00:03:30,836 --> 00:03:31,396
fall.

74
00:03:31,636 --> 00:03:33,879
So why does the Torah place them side

75
00:03:33,959 --> 00:03:34,839
by side?

76
00:03:35,026 --> 00:03:38,679
Rashi answers: to teach that whoever desecrates the

77
00:03:38,751 --> 00:03:42,359
festivals is considered as if they desecrated Shabbat,

78
00:03:42,399 --> 00:03:45,479
and whoever properly observes the festivals is considered

79
00:03:45,532 --> 00:03:47,559
as if they kept Shabbat.

80
00:03:47,692 --> 00:03:50,039
In other words, don't think of the festivals

81
00:03:50,092 --> 00:03:52,159
as somehow lesser than Shabbat.

82
00:03:52,199 --> 00:03:54,759
They carry the same weight and sanctity.

83
00:03:54,946 --> 00:03:57,639
It's a powerful reminder that all of sacred

84
00:03:57,735 --> 00:04:01,879
time, whether divinely fixed or humanly proclaimed, deserves

85
00:04:01,939 --> 00:04:06,173
our full reverence.One of the most emotionally stirring

86
00:04:06,244 --> 00:04:10,173
passages in this portion is Leviticus twenty-two, verses

87
00:04:10,207 --> 00:04:14,173
thirty-two and thirty-three, where God says, "You shall

88
00:04:14,253 --> 00:04:17,373
not profane My holy name, and I shall

89
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:21,853
be sanctified among the children of Israel." Rashi

90
00:04:21,906 --> 00:04:25,133
reads the first part, the prohibition against profaning

91
00:04:25,193 --> 00:04:28,493
God's name, as referring to deliberate transgression.

92
00:04:28,713 --> 00:04:31,933
But then he asks: if we already know

93
00:04:32,013 --> 00:04:34,573
not to profane God's name, what does the

94
00:04:34,635 --> 00:04:37,853
positive command to sanctify God's name add?

95
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:41,293
He answers that it introduces the concept of

96
00:04:41,357 --> 00:04:45,693
kiddush Hashem, the sanctification of God's name, which

97
00:04:45,746 --> 00:04:48,253
in its ultimate form means being willing to

98
00:04:48,317 --> 00:04:51,693
give one's life rather than violate certain core

99
00:04:51,753 --> 00:04:52,653
commandments.

100
00:04:52,933 --> 00:04:56,893
But Rashi adds a remarkable psychological insight.

101
00:04:57,186 --> 00:04:59,773
He says that a person who surrenders to

102
00:04:59,830 --> 00:05:03,213
martyrdom must do so with genuine willingness to

103
00:05:03,333 --> 00:05:06,973
die, not with the secret expectation that God

104
00:05:07,005 --> 00:05:09,453
will perform a miracle and save them.

105
00:05:09,773 --> 00:05:13,853
He brings the example of Chananyah, Mishael, and

106
00:05:13,933 --> 00:05:16,493
Azaryah from the Book of Daniel, who were

107
00:05:16,550 --> 00:05:18,973
thrown into a fiery furnace and declared to

108
00:05:18,993 --> 00:05:22,013
the king: whether God saves us or not,

109
00:05:22,173 --> 00:05:24,493
we will not worship your gods.

110
00:05:24,733 --> 00:05:26,813
That is the standard.

111
00:05:27,069 --> 00:05:31,453
True sanctification of God's name means acting without

112
00:05:31,526 --> 00:05:35,271
conditions.Let's turn now to Sukkot itself.

113
00:05:35,484 --> 00:05:38,472
In verse forty, the Torah commands us to

114
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:40,952
take the fruit of a beautiful tree, a

115
00:05:41,009 --> 00:05:44,632
branch of palm trees, boughs of thick-leaved trees,

116
00:05:44,672 --> 00:05:45,992
and willows of the brook.

117
00:05:46,339 --> 00:05:49,992
Rashi zeroes in on the phrase p'ri eitz

118
00:05:50,052 --> 00:05:52,792
hadar, the fruit of the beautiful tree.

119
00:05:53,032 --> 00:05:55,352
How do we know this refers to the

120
00:05:55,432 --> 00:05:56,872
etrog, the citron?

121
00:05:57,165 --> 00:05:59,832
Rashi offers a beautiful wordplay.

122
00:06:00,072 --> 00:06:03,032
He explains that hadar can be read as

123
00:06:03,139 --> 00:06:07,432
ha-dar, meaning "that which dwells," referring to a

124
00:06:07,496 --> 00:06:09,832
tree whose fruit remains dwelling on the tree

125
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:12,472
from year to year, through all the seasons.

126
00:06:12,632 --> 00:06:15,832
Unlike most fruit trees where the fruit ripens

127
00:06:15,852 --> 00:06:18,472
and falls, the etrog stays on the branch

128
00:06:18,529 --> 00:06:19,992
across multiple years.

129
00:06:20,136 --> 00:06:21,992
That's how we identify it.

130
00:06:22,312 --> 00:06:25,992
It's a lovely example of Rashi using etymology

131
00:06:26,072 --> 00:06:29,352
and agricultural observation to unlock the meaning of

132
00:06:29,392 --> 00:06:33,730
a biblical phrase.Finally, and perhaps most beautifully for

133
00:06:33,750 --> 00:06:36,210
the holiday of Sukkot, Rashi comments on the

134
00:06:36,290 --> 00:06:40,050
very last verse of our reading, Leviticus twenty-three,

135
00:06:40,090 --> 00:06:43,170
verse forty-three, where the Torah explains the reason

136
00:06:43,210 --> 00:06:45,970
for the commandment of dwelling in sukkot, in

137
00:06:46,050 --> 00:06:49,330
booths: "For I made the children of Israel

138
00:06:49,397 --> 00:06:51,730
dwell in sukkot when I brought them out

139
00:06:51,783 --> 00:06:55,010
of Egypt." Rashi tells us that these sukkot

140
00:06:55,042 --> 00:06:57,090
were not literal huts or booths.

141
00:06:57,186 --> 00:06:59,970
They were the Clouds of Glory, the ananei

142
00:07:00,023 --> 00:07:03,890
ha-kavod, the divine clouds that surrounded and protected

143
00:07:03,910 --> 00:07:06,050
the Israelites during their forty years in the

144
00:07:06,108 --> 00:07:07,010
wilderness.

145
00:07:07,138 --> 00:07:09,250
When we sit in the sukkah, we're not

146
00:07:09,282 --> 00:07:11,930
just commemorating primitive desert shelters.

147
00:07:11,997 --> 00:07:15,010
We're reenacting the experience of being enveloped in

148
00:07:15,090 --> 00:07:17,090
God's protective presence.

149
00:07:17,230 --> 00:07:19,810
The fragile walls and leafy roof of the

150
00:07:19,890 --> 00:07:21,970
sukkah are meant to remind us that our

151
00:07:22,034 --> 00:07:24,530
true shelter is not brick and mortar but

152
00:07:24,550 --> 00:07:29,018
the embrace of the Divine.Stepping back from these

153
00:07:29,083 --> 00:07:32,858
individual comments, a few themes emerge in Rashi's

154
00:07:32,898 --> 00:07:34,458
treatment of this portion.

155
00:07:34,631 --> 00:07:38,258
First, there is his relentless precision.

156
00:07:38,331 --> 00:07:43,418
Every pronoun, every unusual spelling, every seemingly redundant

157
00:07:43,509 --> 00:07:46,698
phrase is a doorway into deeper meaning.

158
00:07:46,938 --> 00:07:50,058
Rashi never lets the text just wash over

159
00:07:50,138 --> 00:07:50,538
him.

160
00:07:50,709 --> 00:07:54,218
Second, there is a recurring theme of intention

161
00:07:54,318 --> 00:07:55,658
and sincerity.

162
00:07:55,738 --> 00:07:59,018
Whether it's the intention behind a sacrifice, the

163
00:07:59,065 --> 00:08:02,778
willingness behind martyrdom, or the awareness behind observing

164
00:08:02,818 --> 00:08:06,218
a festival, Rashi insists that what matters is

165
00:08:06,258 --> 00:08:08,858
not just the outward act but the inner

166
00:08:08,938 --> 00:08:09,658
posture.

167
00:08:09,871 --> 00:08:14,458
Third, and most movingly for Sukkot, Rashi reminds

168
00:08:14,511 --> 00:08:18,538
us that Jewish observance is ultimately about relationship.

169
00:08:18,698 --> 00:08:22,218
The festivals are not mere rituals but appointments

170
00:08:22,250 --> 00:08:23,258
with the Divine.

171
00:08:23,398 --> 00:08:25,898
The sukkah is not just a structure but

172
00:08:25,978 --> 00:08:28,538
a memory of God's sheltering love.

173
00:08:28,678 --> 00:08:32,378
And even the seemingly technical laws about sacrifices

174
00:08:32,445 --> 00:08:35,258
point toward a God who is, as Rashi

175
00:08:35,338 --> 00:08:40,072
says, faithful to pay the reward.Thank you so

176
00:08:40,136 --> 00:08:42,312
much for joining me for this episode of

177
00:08:42,392 --> 00:08:43,672
Rashi in Brief.

178
00:08:43,952 --> 00:08:47,272
Wishing you a joyful and meaningful Sukkot, and

179
00:08:47,312 --> 00:08:48,552
I'll see you next week.

